  "query_preview": ".. Currently, a deepening mid-level trough is situated \noff the western U.S. coastline with broad southwesterly flow \npersisting across the Desert Southwest. Deeper moisture has been \nanalyzed further south near the Baja Peninsula, southern CA, and \nsouthern AZ this afternoon with drier conditions further north, \nthough some moisture currently exists across the forecast area with \nPWATs ranging from 0.6-0.8. Later this afternoon, a weak mid-level \ntrough will traverse across UT providing modest ascent resulting in \nisolated to widely scattered showers and thunderstorms across the \nstate, particularly across southwest UT. Storm motions ranging from \n25-30kts from the southwest amidst lower PWATs generally preclude \nmost flooding concerns today, though some localized nuisance \nflooding may be possible if storms track over already saturated \nlocations given how wet it has been as of late. \n\nLater this evening into tomorrow morning, the mid-level trough will \ndeepen markedly and become negatively tilted as it interacts with a \nsouthern stream impulse further south. This will strengthen ascent \nacross the forecast area with increasing PoPs areawide as a result. \nWhile this event is forecast to be a longer duration light to \nmoderate stratiform rainfall event, rainfall totals raise concern \ngiven already saturated soils across much of the state. For most \nvalley locations, 25th percentile amounts range from 0.75-1.25\" with \nmost mountains, southwest UT, and the Wasatch seeing 25th percentile \namounts ranging from 1.50-2.00\". For 75th percentile amounts, this \nincreases notably for lower elevation locations with a range of 1.50-\n2.00\", and 2.50-3.00\" for all specific aforementioned locations. \nWhile spread remains high, we must acknowledge that even the lower \nrange of these rainfall amounts are rather high and could still \nresult in areal flooding.\n\nOn Sunday night, a cold front will sweep across the area serving to \nput an end to most lower elevation precipitation. However, elevated \nmoisture remains in place amidst northwest flow following the front, \nresulting in widespread mountain snowfall across the entire forecast \narea. For areas above 8000ft, around 3-6\" of snowfall is expected \nfrom this system. Further north, primarily concerning the \nCottonwoods and western Uintas above 10000ft, totals will range from \n18-24\" given northwest flow amidst ample moisture remaining in \nplace. \n\nOn Monday, transient ridging builds in the wake of the departing \nsystem bringing cool, dry, and stable conditions to the forecast \narea. Another trough looks to impact the region mid-week bringing \nanother stretch of unsettled weather, though spread remains high at \nthis time making it difficult to iron out specific impacts.",
